    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve for the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\) given that \\(\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2\\), we need to utilize a fundamental property of determinants. Specifically, we use the property that for any square matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), and any integer \\(n\\), the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{M}^n\\) is equal to the \\(n\\)-th power of the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), i.e., \n\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{M}^n) = (\\det \\mathbf{M})^n\n\\]\n\nIn this case, we are given \\(\\det(\\mathbf{M}) = -2\\) and we want to find \\(\\det(\\mathbf{M}^4\\). Using the property mentioned above, we calculate:\n\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{M}^4) = (\\det \\mathbf{M})^4\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given value of the determinant \\(\\mathbf{M}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M}^4 = (-2)^4 = 2^4 = 16\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{M}^4) = (-2)^4 = 16\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).",
